iao:0000409 iao:0000112 """The Basic Formal Ontology ontology makes a distinction between Universals and defined classes, where the formal are \"natural kinds\" and the latter arbitrary collections of entities.""";
            iao:0000115 "A denotator type indicates how a term should be interpreted from an ontological perspective.";
            iao:0000117 "Alan Ruttenberg";
            iao:0000119 "Barry Smith, Werner Ceusters";
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "denotator type";
            rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000102;
            owl:equivalentClass [owl:oneOf (iao:0000410
                                            iao:0000420
                                            iao:0000421) ; 
                                 a owl:Class].
iao:0000410 iao:0000116 """Hard to give a definition for. Intuitively a \"natural kind\" rather than a collection of any old things, which a class is able to be, formally. At the meta level, universals are defined as positives, are disjoint with their siblings, have single asserted parents.""";
            iao:0000117 "Alan Ruttenberg";
            iao:0000119 "A Formal Theory of Substances, Qualities, and Universals, http://ontology.buffalo.edu/bfo/SQU.pdf";
            a iao:0000409,
              owl:NamedIndividual;
            rdfs:label "universal".
