obi:0000006 iao:0000111 "role of being consumer safety officer";
            iao:0000112 "Consumer safety officer; Consumer Safety Officer Positions at FDA http://69.20.19.211/jobs/cso.htm"@en,
                        "Person charged with serving as CSO, FDA official who coordinates the review"@en;
            iao:0000114 iao:0000120;
            iao:0000115 "the role of a human being that is realized by enforcing regulations to ensure consumer safety";
            iao:0000117 "Person:Helen Parkinson",
                        "Jennifer Fostel"@en;
            iao:0000119 "OBI, CDISC"@en;
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "role of being consumer safety officer"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf obi:0000273.
obi:0000010 iao:0000111 "fluorescent reporter intensity"@en;
            iao:0000114 iao:0000125;
            iao:0000115 "A measurement datum that represents the output of a scanner measuring the intensity value for each fluorescent reporter."@en;
            iao:0000117 "person:Chris Stoeckert"@en;
            iao:0000119 "group:OBI";
            iao:0000232 "From the DT branch: This term and definition were originally submitted by the community to our branch, but we thought they best fit DENRIE. However we see several issues with this. First of all the name 'probe' might not be used in OBI. Instead we have a 'reporter' role. Also, albeit the term 'probe intensity' is often used in communities such as the microarray one, the name 'probe' is ambiguous (some use it to refer to what's on the array, some use it to refer to what's hybed to the array). Furthermore, this concept could possibly be encompassed by combining different OBI terms, such as the roles of analyte, detector and reporter (you need something hybed to a probe on the array to get an intensity) and maybe a more general term for 'measuring intensities'. We need to find the right balance between what is consistent with OBI and combinations of its terms and what is user-friendly. Finally, note that 'intensity' is already in the OBI .owl file and is also in PATO. Why didn't OBI import it from PATO? This might be a problem."@en;
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "fluorescent reporter intensity"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000109.
obi:0000011 iao:0000111 "planned process",
                        "planned process"@en;
            iao:0000112 "Injecting mice with a vaccine in order to test its efficacy";
            iao:0000114 iao:0000122;
            iao:0000115 "A processual entity that realizes a plan which is the concretization of a plan specification."@en;
            iao:0000116 """We are only considering successfully completed planned processes. A plan may be modified, and details added during execution. For a given planned process, the associated realized plan specification is the one encompassing all changes made during execution. This means that all processes in which an agent acts towards achieving some 
objectives is a planned process.""",
                        "'Plan' includes a future direction sense. That can be problematic if plans are changed during their execution. There are however implicit contingencies for protocols that an agent has in his mind that can be considered part of the plan, even if the agent didn't have them in mind before. Therefore, a planned process can diverge from what the agent would have said the plan was before executing it, by adjusting to problems encountered during execution (e.g. choosing another reagent with equivalent properties, if the originally planned one has run out.)";
            iao:0000117 "Bjoern Peters";
            iao:0000119 "branch derived";
            iao:0000232 "This class merges the previously separated objective driven process and planned process, as they the separation proved hard to maintain. (1/22/09, branch call)"@en,
                        "6/11/9: Edited at workshop. Used to include: is initiated by an agent";
            iao:0000412 pobo:obi.owl;
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "planned process"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf pobo:BFO_0000015;
            owl:equivalentClass [owl:someValuesFrom [owl:someValuesFrom iao:0000104 ; 
                                                     owl:onProperty pobo:RO_0000059 ; 
                                                     a owl:Restriction] ; 
                                 owl:onProperty pobo:BFO_0000055 ; 
                                 a owl:Restriction].
obi:0000014 iao:0000111 "regulator role";
            iao:0000112 "Fact sheet - Regulating the companies The role of the regulator. Ofwat is the economic regulator of the water and sewerage industry in England and Wales.  http://www.ofwat.gov.uk/aptrix/ofwat/publish.nsf/Content/roleofregulator_factsheet170805"@en;
            iao:0000114 iao:0000120;
            iao:0000115 "a regulatory role involved with making and/or enforcing relevant legislation and governmental orders"@en;
            iao:0000117 "Person:Jennifer Fostel"@en;
            iao:0000118 "regulator";
            iao:0000119 "OBI"@en;
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "regulator role"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf obi:0000017.
obi:0000015 iao:0000111 "biological feature identification objective"@en;
            iao:0000114 iao:0000123;
            iao:0000115 "Biological_feature_identification_objective is an objective role carried out by the proposition defining the aim of a study designed to examine or characterize a particular biological feature."@en;
            iao:0000117 "Jennifer Fostel"@en;
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "biological feature identification objective"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000005.
obi:0000016 iao:0000111 "regulation-assigned role"@en;
            iao:0000112 "Approval letter"@en;
            iao:0000114 iao:0000123;
            iao:0000115 "Regulation-assigned role is a regulatory role defined by legislation or governmental orders"@en;
            iao:0000117 "Person: Jennifer Fostel"@en;
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "regulation-assigned role"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf obi:0000017.
obi:0000017 iao:0000111 "regulatory role"@en;
            iao:0000112 "Regulatory agency, Ethics committee, Approval letter; example:  Browse these EPA Regulatory Role subtopics  http://www.epa.gov/ebtpages/enviregulatoryrole.html  Feb 29, 2008"@en;
            iao:0000114 iao:0000120;
            iao:0000115 "a role which inheres in material entities and is realized in the processes of making, enforcing or being defined by legislation or orders issued by a governmental body."@en;
            iao:0000117 "GROUP:  Role branch"@en;
            iao:0000119 "OBI, CDISC"@en;
            iao:0000232 "govt agents responsible for creating regulations; proxies for enforcing regulations.  CDISC definition:  regulatory authorities. Bodies having the power to regulate. NOTE: In the ICH GCP guideline the term includes the authorities that review submitted clinical data and those that conduct inspections. These bodies are sometimes referred to as competent"@en;
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "regulatory role"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf pobo:BFO_0000023.
obi:0000018 iao:0000111 "material supplier role";
            iao:0000112 "Jackson Labs is an organization which provide mice as experimental material";
            iao:0000114 iao:0000120;
            iao:0000115 "a role realized through the process of supplying materials such as animal subjects, reagents or other materials used in an investigation."@en;
            iao:0000116 "Supplier role is a special kind of service, e.g. biobank";
            iao:0000117 "PERSON:Jennifer Fostel"@en;
            iao:0000118 "material provider role",
                        "supplier";
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "material supplier role"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf obi:0000947.
obi:0000020 iao:0000111 "contract research organization role";
            iao:0000114 iao:0000123;
            iao:0000115 "a worker role of carrying out the study according to the protocol document or study plan delivered by the PI, under the control of the study director.  This role cannot make decisions about the study execution"@en;
            iao:0000117 "Person: Jennifer Fostel"@en;
            iao:0000118 "contract research organization";
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "contract research organization role"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf obi:0000116.
obi:0000021 iao:0000111 "list-mode data file"@en;
            iao:0000112 "An example of a list-mode data file is a file following list-mode Flow Cytometry Standard (FCS) format. Since FCS files can be in histogram mode or  list-mode we have to specify which data format specifically. List-mode format in the overwhelming (even universal) option used."@en;
            iao:0000114 iao:0000125;
            iao:0000115 "A list-mode data file is a binary digital entity where events are stored sequentially, parameter by parameter."@en;
            iao:0000116 "One example of usage is in the context of flow cytometry, however is not restricted to this community and is more widely used, e.g. by imaging people."@en;
            iao:0000117 "person:Chris Stoeckert"@en;
            iao:0000119 "group:Flow Cytometry community";
            a owl:Class;
            rdfs:label "list-mode data file"@en;
            rdfs:subClassOf iao:0000030.
obi:0000023 iao:0000111 "classified data set";
            iao:0000114 iao:0000125;
            iao:0000115 "A data set that is produced as the output of a class prediction data transformation and consists of a data set with assigned class labels."@en;
            iao:0000117 "PERSON: James Malone",
